The two information rates need not match
Define \(k_X\) as the forward rate and \(k_Y\) as the reverse rate:
\[
\begin{aligned}
k_X&=\lambda_X\log\frac{\lambda_X}{\lambda_Y}
+\lambda_Y-\lambda_X,\\
k_Y&=\lambda_Y\log\frac{\lambda_Y}{\lambda_X}
+\lambda_X-\lambda_Y.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
E^X\!\left[\log\frac{X(t)}{X(0)}\right]&=k_Xt,\\
E^Y\!\left[\log\frac{X(t)}{X(0)}\right]&=-k_Yt.
\end{aligned}
\]
Both rates are nonnegative, and both vanish only when the intensities coincide. Unlike the normal example, the divergences are generally asymmetric. The widget controls \(k_X\) and displays the resulting \(k_Y\).

Direction and information
At a driver jump \(\Delta I(t)&gt;0\), the price multiplier is \(e^{a\Delta I(t)}\). If \(\gamma_X&gt;\gamma_Y\), then \(a&lt;0\): greater realized clock activity decreases the price. The deterministic component has \(b&gt;0\). Reversing the parameter ordering reverses both signs.
The two information rates have compact forms:
\[
\begin{aligned}
k_X&=\frac{\delta}{2\gamma_X}
(\gamma_X-\gamma_Y)^2,\\
k_Y&=\frac{\delta}{2\gamma_Y}
(\gamma_X-\gamma_Y)^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore
\[
\begin{aligned}
E^X\!\left[\log\frac{X(t)}{X(0)}\right]&=k_Xt,\\
E^Y\!\left[\log\frac{X(t)}{X(0)}\right]&=-k_Yt.
\end{aligned}
\]
Unless the two laws coincide, the reverse-to-forward information-rate ratio is \(k_Y/k_X=\gamma_X/\gamma_Y\). Matching the forward rate to another family does not generally match the reverse rate or the path shape.
